            "title": "A window where obstructions stay in check",
            "headline": "The local-global obstruction signal is bounded not at a point but across the whole interval [Y, Y²].",
            "explanation": "Cohomological obstructions measure the tension between agreeing locally at every prime and agreeing globally. This piece guarantees that for large scales X there is a window [Y, Y²] inside [X^(1/J), √X] where the weighted total of these obstructions stays within a budget that grows slowly compared with exponential scale. Two streams feed it (the Galois row Row0b and prime pairs), and its output flows to the \"hosts\" layer.",
            "key_insight": "Control over a whole window, not at isolated points, is what the downstream layer needs.",
            "analogy_anchor": "A bridge engineer doesn't need calm winds all year, only a guaranteed weather window to set the span.",
