              "where_metaphor_breaks": "Halls are built by people; here 'hosts' are arithmetic objects whose existence is proved from a hypothesis, and 'far down the road' means all sufficiently large scales, not physical distance."
            }
          },
          "L1": {
            "title": "Window In, Hosts Out",
            "headline": "One upstream signal per character unlocks hosts of fixed width at every large scale.",
            "explanation": "For each primitive character, the cohomology stratum delivers a window condition; this component converts that single signal into a steady supply of arithmetic hosts. The width is fixed at 3/2 and does not drift with the scale, which is what lets the comparison step treat all characters uniformly. The only threshold is a starting scale, past which supply never runs out.",
            "key_insight": "Uniform width across all characters and all large scales is the property the downstream comparison needs.",
            "analogy_anchor": "A supply line: one permit at the gate guarantees standard-size containers at every later station.",
            "loss_meter": {
              "fidelity_score": 0.45,
              "compression_ratio": "~50,000:1",
              "topology_status": "faithful_projection",
              "what_is_omitted": [
