      "statistic": {
        "en": {
          "L0": {
            "title": "The Prime-Drum Noise Meter",
            "headline": "Subtract the beat you expect, and listen only to the wobble that remains.",
            "explanation": "Imagine a huge drum that is struck at special moments, the prime moments, and a metronome that ticks the steady beat we expect. The meter plays the drum and the metronome together so that the expected beat cancels out completely, leaving only the wobble. It also listens to the same drum through a few coloured filters and adds those wobbles with small, equal, never-negative shares. Later parts of the machine must show that this wobble stays quiet.",
            "key_insight": "We don't measure the primes themselves. We measure how far they drift from the expected rhythm, and that drift is the thing that has to stay small.",
            "analogy_anchor": "A drum played against a metronome, heard through a set of coloured filters",
            "loss_meter": {
              "fidelity_score": 0.2,
              "compression_ratio": "~500,000:1",
              "topology_status": "lossy_analogy",
              "what_is_omitted": [
                "The von Mangoldt weights (prime powers count with weight log p)",
                "The Dirichlet characters behind the 'coloured filters', and the exact weight 1/(3o+1)",
                "The ramp smoothing and the Mellin transform passed on to the residue layer",
                "The exact first-moment constant −1−γ"
              ],
              "where_metaphor_breaks": "Primes are positions on the number line, not moments in time. The 'filters' are complex-valued characters, not sound colours. And 'quiet' does not mean silent: the target is a power bound of size about √x, not zero."
            }
          },
          "L1": {
            "title": "A Centered Error Signal with Positive Mixing",
            "headline": "Main terms cancel exactly, so the only thing left is error, and every twisted error enters with a non-negative weight.",
            "explanation": "The statistic is built so that its main terms cancel exactly, which means any size it has comes purely from the error signal of the primes. Several twisted versions over a cyclic family of characters are added with positive weights, so one member's error cannot be hidden by another member's opposite error. The requirement has to hold on whole windows of scales, and the loss exponents must stay fixed while the precision parameter ε shrinks. One anchor is proved without assuming RH: the average error, its first moment, settles to the exact constant −1−γ, which fixes the coefficient of the linear centering term.",
            "key_insight": "Exact centering combined with positive weights turns 'the primes are well-behaved' into one measurable quantity that cannot cancel against itself.",
            "analogy_anchor": "A balance scale zeroed exactly at the expected weight, carrying several pans that are all loaded in the same direction",
            "loss_meter": {
              "fidelity_score": 0.45,
              "compression_ratio": "~50,000:1",
              "topology_status": "faithful_projection",
              "what_is_omitted": [
