                "The definition of tied families and the predicate EFamC",
                "The Mellin/Laurent mechanism that computes the constant −1−γ",
                "The role of the Siegel–Walfisz bound as the dominating function"
              ],
              "where_metaphor_breaks": "'Cannot hide by cancellation' holds only at the level of the outer non-negative weights. Each twisted term Re(gχ) can itself be negative, and only the bound from the comparison layer keeps the total under control."
            }
          },
          "L2": {
            "title": "Contract of the Statistic G_P and the Window Requirement",
            "headline": "Input: the level Λ. Output: G_P(y) and the window predicate that the comparison layer must satisfy.",
            "explanation": "G_P(y) = Gζ(y) + Σ_{ψ∈Nt} wt(ψ)·Re(gχ(ψ.χ, y)), and on a cyclic level every nontrivial primitive inducing character has weight 1/(3o+1). ChebyshevInequalityAtWindowScale(λ, Λ) requires constants eD, eL and ε₁ > 0 such that every 0 < ε < ε₁ admits a tied family with whole windows of width λ satisfying the Chebyshev inequality. The exponents are uniform in ε, while the family may vary with ε. The first-moment anchor is ∫₁^U (ψ(t)−t)/t² dt → −1−γ as U → ∞, and the statistic is passed to the residue layer through a Mellin transform (MellinRamp).",
            "key_insight": "The order of quantifiers matters: eD and eL are fixed before ε, and only the family Fm may depend on ε.",
            "analogy_anchor": "A software interface: a fixed loss budget (eD, eL) and a family implementation that may change with each precision setting ε",
            "loss_meter": {
              "fidelity_score": 0.7,
              "compression_ratio": "~5,000:1",
              "topology_status": "faithful_projection",
              "what_is_omitted": [
                "The internal definitions of gChi, Gz and the ramp smoothing",
                "The structure of Fam/core and the predicate ChebyshevInequalityOnFamilyWindows",
                "The proof steps for the constant −1−γ (Mellin, dominated convergence, Laurent)"
              ],
              "where_metaphor_breaks": "The 'interface' description hides the fact that the window predicate is an existential statement about families. It does not construct any family; the families come from upstream layers."
            }
          },
          "L3": {
            "title": "The Ramp-Weighted von Mangoldt Sum and the First Moment −1−γ",
            "headline": "A Mellin identity for σ > 1, a Siegel–Walfisz remainder bound and the Laurent expansion of ζ′/ζ at s = 1 give an unconditional constant.",
            "explanation": "For real σ > 1: σ∫₁^∞ rem(t)·t^{−(σ+1)} dt = −(ζ′/ζ(σ) + 1/(σ−1)) − 1. This follows from LSeries(Λ) = −ζ′/ζ and ∫₁^∞ t^{−σ} dt = 1/(σ−1). The Siegel–Walfisz bound (sw_bound) gives rem = O(t/log² t), which allows dominated convergence to σ = 1 (core_tendsto). The Laurent expansion ζ′/ζ(s) + 1/(s−1) → γ (zeta_logderiv_limit, via g = (s−1)ζ(s) with g(1) = 1 and g′(1) = γ) then gives ∫₁^∞ (ψ(t)−t)/t² dt = −1−γ.",
            "key_insight": "The constant −1−γ comes from the finite part of the logarithmic derivative of ζ at the pole, without any assumption about zeros. That is why it can calibrate the centering without circular reasoning.",
            "analogy_anchor": "Abelian summation: matching the σ → 1⁺ limit of a Mellin transform with an improper integral",
            "loss_meter": {
              "fidelity_score": 0.9,
              "compression_ratio": "~200:1",
              "topology_status": "faithful_projection",
              "what_is_omitted": [
