        "en": {
          "L0": {
            "title": "An echo nobody can silence",
            "headline": "Every hidden silence in the orchestra leaves a loud echo in the hall.",
            "explanation": "Picture a concert hall where several orchestras play at once, and every musician may only add sound, never take it away. If one orchestra hides a tiny “hole” in its music, the hall answers with a strong, clear echo. Because everyone only adds, nobody can accidentally play an “anti-echo” to cancel it.",
            "key_insight": "When everyone only adds, no trace can vanish by accidental cancelling.",
            "analogy_anchor": "A concert hall with echoes, where musicians can only add sound.",
            "loss_meter": {
              "fidelity_score": 0.2,
              "compression_ratio": "~500,000:1",
              "topology_status": "lossy_analogy",
              "what_is_omitted": [
                "The Mellin transform of a ramp and its form n^{-s}/(s(s-1))",
                "The zero set of the family of Dirichlet L-functions, including conjugate zeros",
                "The notion of a pole and a residue in the complex plane",
                "Growth control of the auxiliary functions (Gchi_growth)"
              ],
              "where_metaphor_breaks": "A real echo fades with distance, while a pole is an exact singular point of a complex function; the “hole in the music” is a zero of an L-function, not an absence of sound."
            }
          },
          "L1": {
            "title": "A zero becomes an alarm that cannot be muted",
            "headline": "Positive weights turn every zero into an unremovable alarm signal.",
            "explanation": "The family's prime statistic is translated into a function on a new “frequency space”, where every zero of any family member becomes a sharp spike — a pole. The spike's strength is a sum of contributions with positive weights, so it cannot be cancelled out. This closes an escape route: a zero cannot hide, it always leaves a measurable trace that is passed on to the criterion.",
            "key_insight": "Positivity of the weights is the safety catch: it turns the existence of a zero into a guaranteed nonzero signal.",
            "analogy_anchor": "An alarm system whose sensors can only raise the alarm level, never lower it.",
            "loss_meter": {
              "fidelity_score": 0.45,
              "compression_ratio": "~50,000:1",
              "topology_status": "faithful_projection",
              "what_is_omitted": [
                "The explicit ramp transform n^{-s}/(s(s-1))",
                "Assembling pole data separately for χ_i and χ_i^{-1} (admissibility condition)",
                "Isolation and integrability conditions inside PoleData",
                "The restriction Re(ρ) > 0 in the zero-set definition"
              ],
              "where_metaphor_breaks": "An alarm is one quantity, but here each zero yields its own pole with its own residue; “nonzero” is a statement about positivity of S(ρ), not about loudness."
            }
          },
          "L2": {
            "title": "Producer of the PoleData contract",
            "headline": "Input: a finite character family; output: a transform Φ with pole data on the zero set.",
            "explanation": "The input interface (MellinRamp) supplies the ramp transform n^{-s}/(s(s-1)); this component assembles transforms of the individual characters χ_i and χ_i^{-1} into one function Φ for the statistic gm F. Output contract: there exists Φ with PoleData (gm F) Φ (zeroSet F), where zeroSet is the set of points with Re > 0 at which L(χ_i, ρ) or L(χ_i, conj ρ) vanishes. The invariant handed to the criterion is S(ρ) > 0 — every pole's residue is nonzero thanks to positive weights.",
            "key_insight": "This component does not prove zero-freeness; it only guarantees that every zero is converted into a detectable pole with positive residue.",
            "analogy_anchor": "A signal-conversion module: events in the zero domain → spikes of guaranteed amplitude in the transform domain.",
            "loss_meter": {
              "fidelity_score": 0.7,
              "compression_ratio": "~5,000:1",
              "topology_status": "faithful_projection",
              "what_is_omitted": [
                "The exact fields of the PoleData structure (integrability, analyticity, poles, isolation)",
                "Details of the per-character lemma poleData_char",
                "The role of the growth constants from Gchi_growth",
                "The proof of conjugation symmetry (hconj)"
              ],
              "where_metaphor_breaks": "“Conversion module” suggests an algorithm; in fact this is an existence theorem (∃ Φ), with no computable procedure for locating poles."
            }
          },
          "L3": {
            "title": "Positive residues of the Mellin-transformed ramp",
            "headline": "The ramp's Mellin transform is n^{-s}/(s(s-1)); zeros of L(χ_i, ·) become poles of Φ whose residues cannot be cancelled.",
            "explanation": "For a finite family F with admissibility (N_i = 1 or χ_i ≠ 1, inherited by χ_i^{-1}), the proof chooses from poleData_char functions Φ1, Φ2 for χ_i and χ_i^{-1} together with integrability, analyticity, pole and isolation data, while Gchi_growth controls growth. The zero set is {ρ : Re ρ > 0, ∃ i, L(χ_i, ρ) = 0 ∨ L(χ_i, ρ̄) = 0}, and conjugation symmetry (hconj) lets the two halves be combined. Positivity of the family weights guarantees the pole coefficient S(ρ) > 0, exactly the premise of the localized Landau theorem.",
            "key_insight": "Positive coefficients transfer a Landau-type argument (nonnegative Dirichlet series) to finite character families: residues cannot cancel.",
            "analogy_anchor": "Landau's classical mechanism: a singularity of a transform with nonnegative coefficients cannot hide.",
            "loss_meter": {
              "fidelity_score": 0.9,
              "compression_ratio": "~200:1",
              "topology_status": "faithful_projection",
              "what_is_omitted": [
                "The full poleData proof beyond lines 248–259 and its auxiliary lemmas",
                "The formal definition of gm F and of CharFamily weights",
                "Exact Gchi_growth bounds and isolation conditions"
              ],
              "where_metaphor_breaks": "“Residue S(ρ) > 0” summarizes the PoleData structure; the exact form of the pole field and pole multiplicities are settled only by the Lean declaration, not by this description."
            }
          },
          "L4": {
            "title": "Deduction.poleData",
            "headline": "theorem poleData (F : CharFamily) : ∃ Φ : ℂ → ℂ, PoleData (gm F) Φ (zeroSet F)",
            "explanation": "Module Development.AutomorphicArithmetic.DedekindLFunctions.LandauTheorems.poleData, lines 248–259, commit 40e56bab026552e089d7afcfeff08d2defb9c005, Lean 4.33.1; node “pole”, kind producer, tier checked. The proof uses classical, poleData_char for χ_i and χ_i^{-1}, Gchi_growth and a rewrite by zeroSet.eq_1; input interface MellinRamp (MellinRamp.lean#L10-L32), output if-10-residues-to-criterion. Declared axioms: propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound.",
            "key_insight": "The theorem is existential and kernel-checked; it is the sole producer of PoleData for the window criterion.",
