                "Special spots where the mirror is blurry and must be checked separately (the point 1, the point 0, the negative even integers)",
                "That the echo may ring on a 'partner' bell (the inverse primitive character), not on the same bell"
              ],
              "where_metaphor_breaks": "A real mirror copies a sound; here a zero at s is sent to 1−s of a possibly different (inverse, primitive) L-function, and the mirror only works after proving that the multiplying factors cannot vanish themselves."
            }
          },
          "L1": {
            "title": "Halving the Battlefield",
            "headline": "Symmetry about the critical line turns a two-sided problem into a one-sided one.",
            "explanation": "The functional equation is a symmetry about the critical line (real part ½): it ties the value at s to the value at 1−s through factors that provably do not vanish inside the strip. So a zero strictly left of the line forces a zero strictly right of it, and the whole burden collapses onto one claim: no zeros right of ½, for every character. Reflection is the last valve — it carries no hard analysis, it only redirects pressure.",
            "key_insight": "All real difficulty lives upstream; this layer only converts right-half-plane silence into on-the-line conclusions.",
            "analogy_anchor": "A pressure valve that routes every left-side leak into the already-sealed right-side chamber.",
            "loss_meter": {
              "fidelity_score": 0.45,
              "compression_ratio": "~50,000:1",
              "topology_status": "faithful_projection",
              "what_is_omitted": [
                "Why the multiplying factors (Gamma, cosine, powers of 2π, gamma factors of characters) cannot vanish",
                "Reduction of an imprimitive character to its primitive inducing character before reflecting",
                "The separate handling of zeta for real part ≤ 0 and ≥ 1"
              ],
              "where_metaphor_breaks": "Symmetry suggests both halves are equally hard; in fact reflection is cheap bookkeeping and offers no evidence that the right half-plane is clear — that comes entirely from the upstream descent via UpperTheory.Top.imprimitive."
            }
          },
          "L2": {
            "title": "Reflection Interface Contract",
            "headline": "One universally quantified nonvanishing hypothesis in; critical-line statements out.",
            "explanation": "Input (HalfPlane / UpperTheory.EveryCharacter, from descent): for every N > 0, every χ mod N and every s with Re s > ½, excluding the principal pole, L(s,χ) ≠ 0. Each reducer splits on the trichotomy Re s <, =, > ½: the right case contradicts the input directly, the left case reduces χ to its primitive χ*, reflects to (χ*)⁻¹ at 1−s (where Re(1−s) > ½) and contradicts the input there. Outputs: Claim.Strip, Claim.Zeta and Mathlib's RiemannHypothesis, handed to the endpoints.",
            "key_insight": "No exponents or parameters are consumed here; the layer is a pure logical reducer with a single dependency.",
            "analogy_anchor": "A router that forwards every left-half query to a right-half oracle that has already answered 'no zero'.",
            "loss_meter": {
              "fidelity_score": 0.7,
              "compression_ratio": "~5,000:1",
              "topology_status": "faithful_projection",
              "what_is_omitted": [
