          "L3": {
            "title": "Functional Equations and Primitive Reflection",
            "headline": "Completed L-functions, nonvanishing gamma factors and conductor-preserving inversion move zeros from Re s < ½ to Re s > ½.",
            "explanation": "For ζ, rh_of_right uses riemannZeta_one_sub, ζ(s) = 2(2π)^{−(1−s)} Γ(1−s) cos(π(1−s)/2) ζ(1−s): for Re s < ½, Γ(1−s) ≠ 0 since Re(1−s) > 0, and cos(π(1−s)/2) = 0 would force s = −2k, excluded by s ≠ 0 or the trivial-zero hypothesis. For Dirichlet χ, critLine and prim_reflect write L = Λ/γ, use gammaFactor_ne_zero (Gammaℝ nonvanishing for Re > 0 in even and odd cases), conductor_inv to keep χ⁻¹ primitive, and IsPrimitive.completedLFunction_one_sub to get L(1−s, χ⁻¹) = 0. Claim.strip reaches arbitrary χ via prim_zero to χ.primitiveCharacter, and Claim.zeta handles Re s ≤ 0 by reflecting completedRiemannZeta into Re ≥ 1, where riemannZeta_ne_zero_of_one_le_re applies without the hypothesis.",
            "key_insight": "Primitivity is essential: only primitive characters have the clean completed functional equation, so imprimitive zeros in the strip are first transferred to the inducing character.",
