      "endpoints": {
        "en": {
          "L0": {
            "title": "Five Locks on One Door",
            "headline": "At the end of the treasure hunt, five locks say exactly what was found.",
            "explanation": "At the end of a long treasure hunt stands a door with five locks, each with a sign saying exactly what it guards. Two locks guard the oldest, most famous secret; the other three guard a whole family of related secrets, one for every possible lock size. The keys were all made in the workshop behind the door — the door itself only checks that each key fits.",
            "key_insight": "The door adds no new key; it only shows, in plain words, what the keys open.",
            "analogy_anchor": "A door with five labelled locks at the end of a treasure hunt.",
            "loss_meter": {
              "fidelity_score": 0.2,
              "compression_ratio": "~500,000:1",
              "topology_status": "lossy_analogy",
              "what_is_omitted": [
                "Who made the keys and how (the entire upstream proof chain, ending in reflection)",
                "What the secrets say mathematically (zeros of zeta and Dirichlet L-functions lie on the line Re s = ½)",
                "That two of the locks open with literally the same key (rhOfAll)"
              ],
              "where_metaphor_breaks": "A turning lock only shows the key fits that lock's shape; here 'fitting' means a kernel typechecking a proof against Mathlib's statement, and whether the sign on the lock describes the classical secret correctly is a separate, human question."
            }
          },
          "L1": {
            "title": "The Summit Register",
            "headline": "Ordinary RH and Dirichlet GRH, written in the exact words the outside world uses.",
            "explanation": "This component adds no new force; it is the summit log where results are recorded in the official phrasing. Ordinary RH appears in Mathlib's own wording and again in expanded form, while Dirichlet GRH appears three ways: no zeros right of ½, all open-strip zeros on the line, and a primitive-character version. All five are thin aliases onto upstream proof bundles (rhOfAll, allChi, claimAll, critAll).",
            "key_insight": "Trust concentrates on the statements: if the definitions read right, the endpoints mean what they say.",
            "analogy_anchor": "A summit logbook with five entries signed by three climbing teams.",
            "loss_meter": {
              "fidelity_score": 0.45,
              "compression_ratio": "~50,000:1",
              "topology_status": "faithful_projection",
              "what_is_omitted": [
                "How rhOfAll, allChi, claimAll and critAll are themselves assembled from the reflection layer",
                "The exclusion conditions (trivial zeros, the pole at s = 1, the principal character)",
                "The dedicated primitive reflection argument behind critAll"
              ],
              "where_metaphor_breaks": "A summit log implies one climb to one peak; here five statements share four proof terms, and the 'peak' is only as meaningful as the Mathlib and Statements.lean definitions it is written against."
            }
          },
          "L2": {
            "title": "Exported Theorem Contracts",
            "headline": "Five exports, each typed by a Prop from Mathlib or Statements.lean, with no parameters left.",
            "explanation": "Main.lean exports RiemannHypothesis (Mathlib), RiemannZetaZerosOnLine (ζ(s) = 0, s not of the form −2(n+1), s ≠ 1 → Re s = ½), DirichletLNonvanishingRightOfHalf (∀ N > 0, χ mod N, Re s > ½, χ ≠ 1 ∨ s ≠ 1 → L(s,χ) ≠ 0), DirichletLZerosOnLine (0 < Re s < 1, L(s,χ) = 0 → Re s = ½) and PrimitiveDirichletLZerosOnLine. The invariant is coverage: quantifiers range over every positive modulus N and every character χ, not a finite sample. This layer is pure interface — inbound if-14 delivers the critical line via Claim.zeta · Claim.strip.",
            "key_insight": "Everything checkable here is the shape of the statements; the proofs are one-line references.",
            "analogy_anchor": "A public API header file whose implementations live in other modules.",
            "loss_meter": {
              "fidelity_score": 0.7,
              "compression_ratio": "~5,000:1",
              "topology_status": "faithful_projection",
              "what_is_omitted": [
                "The bodies of rhOfAll, allChi, claimAll and critAll",
                "The exact text of PrimitiveDirichletLZerosOnLine (not excerpted in this capsule)",
                "An audit of the Statements.lean definitions against classical textbook formulations"
              ],
              "where_metaphor_breaks": "API-contract language suggests runtime-checkable guarantees; these are logical propositions over uncountably many s, validated by a type checker, not by test inputs."
            }
          },
          "L3": {
            "title": "RH and Dirichlet GRH as Stated",
            "headline": "Nontrivial zeros of ζ and of every Dirichlet L(s,χ) in the open strip lie on Re s = ½.",
            "explanation": "Ordinary RH: every zero of ζ other than s = −2, −4, … and s = 1 has Re s = ½, stated both as Mathlib's RiemannHypothesis and in expanded form. Dirichlet GRH covers all characters of every positive modulus, including imprimitive and principal ones: L(s,χ) ≠ 0 for Re s > ½ away from the principal pole, and open-strip zeros lie on Re s = ½, with the primitive version proved through a dedicated reflection (critAll). Extra zeros of imprimitive L(s,χ) coming from removed Euler factors lie on Re s = 0, outside the open strip, so the strip statement is consistent with them.",
            "key_insight": "The right-of-½ nonvanishing export is the strongest form; the strip and zeta statements follow from it by reflection.",
            "analogy_anchor": "Classical RH plus GRH for Dirichlet L-functions, in Mathlib's formal vocabulary.",
            "loss_meter": {
              "fidelity_score": 0.9,
              "compression_ratio": "~200:1",
              "topology_status": "faithful_projection",
              "what_is_omitted": [
                "The upstream machinery (Stepanov over Z, unitary theta chains, Behrend sets, Row0b, VWinAt, TopG, Mellin ramp, localized Landau) that the endpoints only inherit",
                "Corollaries that are not exported (prime-counting error terms, explicit-formula consequences)",
                "Any extension beyond Dirichlet characters (Dedekind or automorphic L-functions), which is not claimed"
              ],
              "where_metaphor_breaks": "Domain vocabulary treats 'RH' as the classical statement; the endpoints prove Mathlib's formalization of it, and equivalence to the textbook statement rests on Mathlib's definitions of riemannZeta and DirichletCharacter.LFunction and on a human reading of Statements.lean."
            }
          },
          "L4": {
            "title": "Endpoints — Lean Anchors",
            "headline": "Main.lean L4–L12 at commit 40e56bab026552e089d7afcfeff08d2defb9c005, all five exports tier two-kernels.",
            "explanation": "riemannHypothesis : RiemannHypothesis := rhOfAll (L4); riemannZeta_eq_zero_re : RiemannZetaZerosOnLine := rhOfAll (L6); LFunction_ne_zero_of_half_lt_re : DirichletLNonvanishingRightOfHalf := allChi (L8); LFunction_eq_zero_re : DirichletLZerosOnLine := claimAll.2.1 (L10); LFunction_eq_zero_re_of_isPrimitive : PrimitiveDirichletLZerosOnLine := critAll (L12). Statement definitions live in Statements.lean L3–4, L6–8, L10–12 (tier read); inbound interface if-14 from reflection is Claim.zeta · Claim.strip (ClaimStrip.lean L59–100). Elaborated under Lean 4.33.1 with the standard axioms propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound as reported for this capsule.",
            "key_insight": "Thirteen nodes, five exported theorems, four proof terms — the formal endpoint of the whole DAG.",
            "analogy_anchor": "src/Main.lean#L4-L12 @ 40e56bab",
            "loss_meter": {
              "fidelity_score": 1.0,
              "compression_ratio": "1:1 (formal anchor)",
              "topology_status": "exact_isomorphism",
              "what_is_omitted": [],
              "where_metaphor_breaks": "Formalization Gap / Specification Boundary: two-kernel replay certifies that these terms typecheck against the stated Props; it does not certify that Mathlib's RiemannHypothesis and the Statements.lean definitions faithfully express the classical conjectures, that the kernels are bug-free, or that the full argument has completed human mathematical peer review, which is ongoing."
