                "Dirichlet L-functions and their zeros"
              ],
              "where_metaphor_breaks": "\"Tuning\" and \"test\" suggest measurements one could simply perform; TopG and VWinAt are precise formal predicates that nobody has proved, and there are infinitely many sections (primitive characters)."
            }
          },
          "L1": {
            "title": "One global hypothesis plus per-character windows yield RH and GRH",
            "statement": "Bridge: if hypothesis TopG holds and every primitive Dirichlet character ψ satisfies the window condition VWinAt ψ 401 (the title calls these windows cohomological), then both the Riemann Hypothesis and the Generalized Riemann Hypothesis for Dirichlet L-functions hold. The implication is a Lean theorem; the premises are open and human review is ongoing.",
            "intuition": "Architecture of \"one shared pillar plus infinitely many local checks\". Unlike cut-zeta, the output covers the whole family of L-functions, not just ζ: both endpoints (RH and Dirichlet GRH) leave through a single gate.",
            "loss_meter": {
              "fidelity_score": 0.45,
              "compression_ratio": "~10^4:1",
              "topology_status": "faithful_projection",
              "what_is_omitted": [
