        "en": {
          "title": "Find the mathematical estimate",
          "question": "How wide must the intervals be, and where does the loss in the exponent come from?",
          "step_notes": [
            {
              "component_id": "choreography",
              "note_by_level": {
                "L0": "First decide how much wobble is allowed, and only then choose how closely to look.",
                "L1": "The loss exponents are fixed first. Given a zero with β > ½, choose θ ∈ (½, β), then ε with ½+9ε ≤ θ. No bound at ε = 0 is ever needed.",
                "L2": "Inputs: the G_P bound (if-08) and the criterion (if-11). Output: ZeroFreeAll for primitive characters (if-12). The windows are [Y, Y^(1+δ)], arbitrarily late.",
                "L3": "Quantifier order: e_D and e_L are fixed, then θ ∈ (½, β), then ε ≤ (θ−½)/9. The criterion only needs windows [Y, Y^(1+δ)] for arbitrarily large Y.",
                "L4": "Nodes `zero-top`, `window-consumer`. Edge `zeroFreeAll_of_topG` (ZeroFreeAllOfTopG.lean#L26-L34 @40e56ba)."
              }
            },
            {
              "component_id": "comparison",
              "note_by_level": {
                "L0": "The scale nearly balances, and the small loss is counted exactly.",
                "L1": "The loss in the exponent is (e_D+e_L+7)ε: part comes from the family's geometry, and the constant 7 is part of the formula.",
                "L2": "Inputs: TopG (if-06) and hosts of width 3/2 (if-07). Output: G_P ≤ x^(½+9ε) (if-08).",
                "L3": "Finite Fourier orthogonality on a subgroup and an exact main-term identity give the exponent ½+(e_D+e_L+7)ε. The IR does not decompose the constant 7.",
                "L4": "Nodes `prime-bound`, `core-gm`. Edges: `UpperTheory.Top.supplyChi` (SupplyChi.lean#L8-L16) and `level_gm_le_on_family_scales` (LevelGmLeOnFamilyScales.lean#L28-L30) @40e56ba."
              }
            },
            {
              "component_id": "family",
              "note_by_level": {
                "L0": "The chain is built so that its wobble is always the same number.",
                "L1": "About √x theta lifts, with e_D = 2 and e_L = 0 fixed whatever ε is. That is why the loss does not grow.",
                "L2": "Input: definite-side data (if-04). Output: TopG (if-06).",
                "L3": "Divisibility, slope and covolume control in the theta-lift chain fix e_D = 2 and e_L = 0.",
                "L4": "Nodes `shape`, `family-design`. Edge `UpperTheory.Top.TopG` (ZeroFreeAllOfTopG.lean#L12-L34 @40e56ba)."
              }
            }
          ]
        }
